## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to unpack bit-packed fields without branches—a common low-level task in networking, codecs, and embedded systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Bitwise masks and shifts** extract subfields from packed integers efficiently
- **No branches** ensures constant-time execution, critical for performance-sensitive code
- **Endianness** is irrelevant when masking at exact bit positions

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t  version;
    uint8_t  type;
    uint16_t length;
    uint8_t  flags;
} Header;

Header extractHeaderFields(uint32_t w) {
    Header h;
    h.version = (w >> 28) & 0xF;  // Bits 31-28
    h.type    = (w >> 24) & 0xF;  // Bits 27-24
    h.length  = (w >> 8)  & 0xFFFF; // Bits 23-8
    h.flags   = w & 0xFF;          // Bits 7-0
    return h;
}
```

Each shift moves the target field to the least significant position, then the mask isolates the relevant bits. This approach is branchless, deterministic, and executes in constant time.

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new angle: 128-bit popcount via per-half 64-bit parallel counting using a fixed, loopless sequence of masks.

## Key Concepts

- Bit-twiddling for popcount
- Handling 128-bit values by splitting into 64-bit halves
- No branches or loops; purely arithmetic masks

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.lo) + popcount64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for 256-bit using the same pattern? 
- What are the trade-offs of inlining vs. a dedicated intrinsic on your compiler/architecture?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses branchless bit-twiddling across multiword data and solid understanding of SWAR popcount. 

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (single-instruction, multiple-data) population count
- 64-bit limb handling within a 128-bit composite
- Branchless design using masks and shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t loCount = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  uint64_t b = x.hi;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t hiCount = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  return (int)(loCount + hiCount);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize for wide vectors (e.g., 256-bit) with minimal branching?
- How would you validate correctness across edge cases like zero and all-ones?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests constant-time, multi-word popcount without branches. Bridges knowledge of SWAR patterns from 64-bit into 128-bit by handling halves separately. It also checks handling of non-power-of-two widths.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR/popcount on 64-bit
- 128-bit word handling (hi/lo)
- Branch-free, mask-shift arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t countSetBits64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return countSetBits64(x.lo) + countSetBits64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
