- How to verify edge cases (all-ones, zero) across both halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless 128-bit rotation using only shifts.

## Key Concepts
- View 128-bit value via 128-bit type
- Branchless rotation with shifts and modulo
- Correct unpacking into hi/lo

## Code Example
```c
void rotateRight128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  unsigned int kk = k & 127;
  __uint128_t v = (((__uint128_t)hi) << 64) | lo;
  v = (v >> kk) | (v << ((128 - kk) % 128));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(v >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)v;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement without 128-bit type support?
- What are performance trade-offs for different compilers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked

Tests compact, deterministic bit-trickery without loops, showing knowledge of parallel popcount progressions.

## Key Concepts
- Masks for pairwise sums
- Shifts to propagate counts
- Edge-case: 0 yields 0

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 64-bit inputs without 128-bit math?
- How to test exhaustively across 32-bit space?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 128-bit SWAR techniques and per-byte manipulation, not simply whole-value reversal.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR bit-reversal
- Mask design across 16 bytes
- Branch-free parallel bit-twiddling

## Code Example

```c
uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x){
  const uint128 M1 = ...; // 0x5555... across 128 bits
  const uint128 M2 = ...; // 0x3333...
  const uint128 M4 = ...; // 0x0F0F...
  // stage 1: swap adj bits
  x = (x >> 1) & M1 | (x & M1) << 1;
  // stage 2: swap bit-pairs
  x = (x >> 2) & M2 | (x & M2) << 2;
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x >> 4) & M4 | (x & M4) << 4;
  // stage 4: reverse within bytes
  // (use an appropriate mask like M8)
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for endianness in memory when reading/writing?
- What are the performance implications on CPUs lacking 128-bit intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reduce a 128-bit value to a single parity bit using only bitwise ops, no branching.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based parity reduction
- Cross word reduction
- Constant-time bit twiddling

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return (uint8_t)(x & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for streaming 64-bit chunks?
- Could you optimize for a specific ISA (eg, AVX2) for parity reduction?

## Why This Is Asked

- Checks 128-bit handling and branchless bit counting, a common crypto/DB pattern.
- Evaluates ability to compose primitives without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Splitting 128-bit into hi/lo and aggregating results
- Constant-time, no branches

## Code Example

```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

- Tests ability to implement a canonical bit-counting trick without loops, emphasizing constant-time operations. 
- Demonstrates careful use of masks and shifts to aggregate counts across bit groups.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (parallel bit counting)
- Bit masks 0x55555555, 0x33333333, 0x0F0F0F0F
- Constant-time arithmetic with shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
function popcount32(x) {
  x = x - ((x >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (x * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to 64-bit inputs?
- Compare this approach to using hardware popcount instructions in terms of portability and performance.

## Why This Is Asked
Test ability to count bits with a constant-time sequence of masks and shifts, no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting
- Fixed masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F...
- No branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function popcount64(x) {
  // x is a BigInt
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform fixed-bit manipulations without loops, using SWAR-style packing to reduce 64 bits to 32 bits in one pass.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR parallel bit extraction
- Fixed-mask chain for bit packing
- No branching for deterministic performance

## Code Example

```c
uint32 compressEvenBits64(uint64 x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    return (uint32)x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to extract the odd bits instead of even bits?
- What are the trade-offs vs a loop-based approach on various architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend classic bit-trick patterns to wider word sizes without branches, a common need in performance-critical systems.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 128-bit data layout with two 64-bit halves
- Branchless bit counting and aggregation

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

int popcount128(uint128 a) {
  return (int)(popcount64(a.hi) + popcount64(a.lo));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIMD-enabled languages (e.g., intrinsics)?
- Compare branchless approach vs. lookup-based methods on large datasets.

## Why This Is Asked

The parity check is a common error-detection primitive in data blocks and crypto pipelines. This task tests ability to perform a full 128-bit reduction with pure bitwise ops, no branches, and constant time.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value represented as hi/lo.
- Parity reduction via XOR folding.
- Branchless implementation for performance.

## Code Example

```javascript
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return (int)(x & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt parity128 for native 128-bit ints on platforms with __int128?
- What are the trade-offs if you can't rely on unsigned shifts across all compilers?
